 On apply, TA prints one `[applied]` or `[failed]` line per proposal. Proposals are replayed only when a person is present: from an interactive terminal, or when you pass `--replay-actions`. A non-interactive apply (a script, CI, the session auto-apply gate, the release pipeline) applies the files, prints how many external actions were not replayed, and leaves them for you to run with `ta draft apply <id> --replay-actions`. `--no-replay-actions`, or `TA_NO_REPLAY_ACTIONS=1`, turns replay off even in a terminal. `ta draft apply <id> --chain --dry-run` previews the whole chain and changes and replays nothing. A failed proposal never blocks the others or the file changes; fix the cause and re-run `ta draft apply`. Re-applying is safe: replayed proposals are recorded in `.ta/wayfinder-task-replay.log` and skipped next time, and created tasks always carry an `external_id` (derived from the goal and proposal ids when the agent did not supply one), so a task is never created twice. A project without `[plan] backend = "wayfinder"` simply skips the proposals with a log line. The Wayfinder service account needs only `member` role for these calls.
 
+#### Proposing Wayfinder Goals, KPI Metrics and Datapoints in a Draft
+
+The same review path covers Wayfinder planning. A project-manager worker proposes goals and KPIs with four more tools, and they ride in the draft a person reviews. The chief of staff never writes, and none of these tools exist in chat mode.
+
+| Tool | What it proposes | Required inputs | Applied as |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| `ta_propose_goal_create` | A goal | `name`; optional `description`, `kind` (default `milestone`), `parent_goal_id`, `target_date` (`YYYY-MM-DD`) | `POST /api/projects/<project>/goals` |
+| `ta_propose_kpi_metric_create` | A KPI metric under a goal | `goal_id`, `label`, `unit`, `target`, `cadence` (`none`, `daily`, `weekly`, `monthly`), `kind` (`task_count`, `external`); optional `aggregation` (`sum`, `latest`), `require_gate`, `weight` | `POST .../goals/<goal>/kpi-metrics` |
+| `ta_propose_kpi_datapoint_add` | A datapoint on a metric | `metric_id`, `value`; optional `source` (default `manual`) | `POST .../kpi-metrics/<metric>/datapoints` |
+| `ta_propose_kpi_create` | An org-level KPI | `label`; optional `description`, `weight`, `project_scoped` | `POST /api/orgs/<org>/kpis` |
+
+Every call also takes `goal_run_id`. Numbers must be finite and within plus or minus 1e12, enums must match the values above, and text lengths are capped. A bad proposal is rejected when the agent makes it, so the reviewer never sees it.
+
+**Building a chain with `ref:<n>`.** A goal or metric that does not exist yet has no id. Instead of an id, a later proposal can use `ref:<n>`, where `<n>` is the step number of an earlier goal or metric proposal in the same draft. The tool result tells the agent which `ref:<n>` to use. For example: step 0 creates a goal, step 1 creates a metric with `goal_id` set to `ref:0`, and step 2 adds a datapoint with `metric_id` set to `ref:1`. A ref must point backwards, at the right kind of proposal, and never at a `task_count` metric (Wayfinder computes those, so they take no datapoints). A `ref:<n>` can also be used as `parent_goal_id`.
+
+**What a reviewer sees.** `ta draft view <draft-id>` lists each proposal under pending actions in plain words, such as the goal name and kind, the metric label, unit and target, or the datapoint value. It also shows the step number, the `ref:<n>` the proposal resolves to, and its `external_id`. Nothing is written to Wayfinder until the draft is approved and applied. Rejecting or discussing a step skips it, and any step that refers to it is blocked.
+
+**What apply does.** Steps replay in the order they were proposed, using the project from `[plan.wayfinder]` in `.ta/workflow.toml`. Each create sends `external_id` `ta_draft:<draft-id>:<n>`, so repeating a step updates the same object instead of duplicating it. The id Wayfinder returns is recorded in `.ta/wayfinder-task-replay.log` next to the action id, which is how `ref:<n>` still resolves when you re-run `ta draft apply` after a partial failure: finished steps are skipped, failed ones are retried. If a step fails, every step that refers to it is stopped and the message names the step that blocked it.
+
+Errors name the route, the status and the fix:
+
+- 403 names the missing right: `tasks:write` for goals, metrics and datapoints, `kpis:manage` for org KPIs.
+- 409 is reported as a conflict, with Wayfinder's own message. A datapoint sent to a `task_count` metric is refused this way. Change the proposal, then re-run `ta draft apply`.
+
